    {
      "content": "<h3>Neighbours: lower, floor, ceiling and higher</h3>\n<p>The reason people choose a TreeSet over a HashSet. Given a value, find the closest elements <strong>even if the value itself is not in the set</strong>.</p>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>5. lower, floor, ceiling and higher</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>E lower(E e);  E floor(E e);  E ceiling(E e);  E higher(E e)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> the neighbour, or <code>null</code> if there is none</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Four \"nearest element\" questions: <code>lower(e)</code> = the greatest element <strong>strictly below</strong> e; <code>floor(e)</code> = the greatest element <strong>below or equal</strong>; <code>ceiling(e)</code> = the smallest element <strong>above or equal</strong>; <code>higher(e)</code> = the smallest element <strong>strictly above</strong>. Memory trick: <em>floor</em> and <em>lower</em> look down; <em>ceiling</em> and <em>higher</em> look up; <em>floor/ceiling</em> may return e itself.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Each is one root-to-leaf walk. They are what make TreeSet useful for \"find the next time slot\", \"nearest price\", \"which range does this value fall in\". e does not have to be in the set.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet lower / floor / ceiling / higher\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetNeighboursDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<Integer> altitudes = new TreeSet<>(List.of(100, 250, 400, 650, 900));\n\n        int probe = 400;                                   // present in the set\n        System.out.println(\"For \" + probe + \": lower=\" + altitudes.lower(probe) + \" floor=\" + altitudes.floor(probe)\n                + \" ceiling=\" + altitudes.ceiling(probe) + \" higher=\" + altitudes.higher(probe));\n\n        probe = 500;                                       // NOT in the set\n        System.out.println(\"For \" + probe + \": lower=\" + altitudes.lower(probe) + \" floor=\" + altitudes.floor(probe)\n                + \" ceiling=\" + altitudes.ceiling(probe) + \" higher=\" + altitudes.higher(probe));\n\n        System.out.println(\"Below the smallest: \" + altitudes.lower(100));      // null: nothing smaller\n        System.out.println(\"Above the largest : \" + altitudes.higher(900));     // null: nothing bigger\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// For 400: lower=250 floor=400 ceiling=400 higher=650\n// For 500: lower=400 floor=400 ceiling=650 higher=650\n// Below the smallest: null\n// Above the largest : null"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Range views: headSet, tailSet and subSet</h3>\n<p>Take a part of the set by value. The result is a <strong>live view</strong> of the same data.</p>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>6. headSet, tailSet and subSet</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>SortedSet<E> headSet(E to);  NavigableSet<E> headSet(E to, boolean inclusive);  tailSet(from[, inclusive]);  subSet(from, to) / subSet(from, fromInc, to, toInc)</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a view of that range</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(log n) to create</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> <code>headSet(to)</code> = everything <strong>before</strong> to; <code>tailSet(from)</code> = everything <strong>from</strong> from onwards; <code>subSet(from, to)</code> = from (included) up to to (<strong>excluded</strong>). The longer forms let you choose whether each end is included.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> These are <strong>views</strong>, not copies: changing the view changes the set and vice versa, and adding an element outside the range to the view throws <code>IllegalArgumentException</code>. Wrap in <code>new TreeSet&lt;&gt;(view)</code> for an independent copy.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet headSet / tailSet / subSet: range views\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.NavigableSet;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetRangeDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        NavigableSet<String> forts = new TreeSet<>(List.of(\"Lohagad\", \"Panhala\", \"Purandar\", \"Raigad\", \"Sinhagad\", \"Torna\"));\n\n        System.out.println(\"headSet(Raigad)           : \" + forts.headSet(\"Raigad\"));              // before Raigad\n        System.out.println(\"headSet(Raigad, true)     : \" + forts.headSet(\"Raigad\", true));        // including Raigad\n        System.out.println(\"tailSet(Raigad)           : \" + forts.tailSet(\"Raigad\"));              // Raigad and after\n        System.out.println(\"subSet(Panhala, Sinhagad) : \" + forts.subSet(\"Panhala\", \"Sinhagad\"));  // from included, to excluded\n\n        forts.subSet(\"Panhala\", \"Raigad\").clear();                  // removing through the view changes the set\n        System.out.println(\"After clearing the range  : \" + forts);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// headSet(Raigad)           : [Lohagad, Panhala, Purandar]\n// headSet(Raigad, true)     : [Lohagad, Panhala, Purandar, Raigad]\n// tailSet(Raigad)           : [Raigad, Sinhagad, Torna]\n// subSet(Panhala, Sinhagad) : [Panhala, Purandar, Raigad]\n// After clearing the range  : [Lohagad, Raigad, Sinhagad, Torna]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>Reverse order and iteration</h3>\n<p>Walk the set from the end, or look at it upside down.</p>"
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>7. descendingSet(), descendingIterator() and reversed()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>NavigableSet<E> descendingSet();  Iterator<E> descendingIterator();  NavigableSet<E> reversed()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> a reversed view / iterator</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(1)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> <code>descendingSet()</code> shows the same elements from largest to smallest, as a live view. <code>descendingIterator()</code> walks them that way. In Java 21 <code>reversed()</code> does the same as <code>descendingSet()</code>.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Views are free (no copying). Because it is a <code>NavigableSet</code> itself, you can chain operations, for example <code>set.descendingSet().headSet(x)</code>.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet descendingSet / descendingIterator / reversed\nimport java.util.Iterator;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetDescendingDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<String> forts = new TreeSet<>(List.of(\"Raigad\", \"Sinhagad\", \"Torna\", \"Purandar\"));\n\n        System.out.println(\"ascending     : \" + forts);\n        System.out.println(\"descendingSet : \" + forts.descendingSet());\n        System.out.println(\"reversed (21) : \" + forts.reversed());\n\n        Iterator<String> it = forts.descendingIterator();\n        System.out.print(\"iterator      : \");\n        while (it.hasNext()) {\n            System.out.print(it.next() + \" \");\n        }\n        System.out.println();\n\n        forts.add(\"Lohagad\");                                      // views are live\n        System.out.println(\"view updated  : \" + forts.descendingSet());\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// ascending     : [Purandar, Raigad, Sinhagad, Torna]\n// descendingSet : [Torna, Sinhagad, Raigad, Purandar]\n// reversed (21) : [Torna, Sinhagad, Raigad, Purandar]\n// iterator      : Torna Sinhagad Raigad Purandar \n// view updated  : [Torna, Sinhagad, Raigad, Purandar, Lohagad]"
      ]
    },
    {
      "content": "<h3>8. iterator, forEach, stream and comparator()</h3>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Signature:</strong> <code>Iterator<E> iterator();  void forEach(Consumer);  Stream<E> stream();  Comparator<? super E> comparator()</code></li>\n<li><strong>Returns:</strong> elements in sorted order / the ordering in use</li>\n<li><strong>Time:</strong> O(n)</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>For freshers:</strong> Iteration, <code>forEach</code> and streams all visit the elements in <strong>sorted order</strong>. <code>comparator()</code> returns the Comparator that defines the order, or <code>null</code> when natural ordering is used.</p>\n<p><strong>For experienced developers:</strong> Streams from a TreeSet are <code>SORTED</code> and <code>DISTINCT</code>, so <code>stream().sorted()</code> is free and <code>distinct()</code> is redundant. The iterator is fail-fast.</p>",
      "codeExamples": [
        "// TreeSet iteration is sorted; comparator() reveals the ordering\nimport java.util.Comparator;\nimport java.util.List;\nimport java.util.TreeSet;\n\npublic class TreeSetIterationDemo {\n    public static void main(String[] args) {\n        TreeSet<String> natural = new TreeSet<>(List.of(\"Torna\", \"Raigad\", \"Sinhagad\"));\n        TreeSet<String> byLength = new TreeSet<>(Comparator.comparing(String::length).thenComparing(Comparator.naturalOrder()));\n        byLength.addAll(natural);\n\n        natural.forEach(f -> System.out.print(f + \" \"));\n        System.out.println();\n        System.out.println(\"stream upper : \" + natural.stream().map(String::toUpperCase).toList());\n\n        System.out.println(\"natural.comparator()  : \" + natural.comparator());              // null means natural ordering\n        System.out.println(\"byLength.comparator() is set: \" + (byLength.comparator() != null));\n        System.out.println(\"byLength order        : \" + byLength);\n    }\n}\n// OUTPUT:\n// Raigad Sinhagad Torna \n// stream upper : [RAIGAD, SINHAGAD, TORNA]\n// natural.comparator()  : null\n// byLength.comparator() is set: true\n// byLength order        : [Torna, Raigad, Sinhagad]"
      ]
    }
